WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.283
Look at a mountain, a valley and a plain.

00:00:02.513 --> 00:00:05.627
They seem permanent, yet Earth's surface keeps changing.

00:00:05.857 --> 00:00:12.513
A landform is a feature of the solid surface, and the arrangement of landforms in an area is its topography.

00:00:12.743 --> 00:00:18.311
Land covers about twenty-nine percent of Earth's surface, with water covering most of the rest.

00:00:19.061 --> 00:00:21.643
Two sets of processes shape this surface.

00:00:21.873 --> 00:00:25.222
Internal, or endogenous, forces act from within Earth.

00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:31.233
They can lift or lower parts of the crust, fold rock layers, produce faults and feed volcanoes.

00:00:31.463 --> 00:00:35.559
They help build major relief features such as mountains and plateaus.

00:00:36.309 --> 00:00:39.720
External, or exogenous, forces work at the surface.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:45.454
Moving water, ice and wind wear rock away through erosion and carry material elsewhere.

00:00:45.684 --> 00:00:49.140
When that material settles, deposition builds new surfaces.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.205
People also reshape land.

00:00:51.435 --> 00:00:57.920
Keep this pair in mind: internal forces create relief, while external forces wear it down and rebuild it.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.857
A continent is a very large land mass, mostly surrounded by ocean.

00:01:04.087 --> 00:01:09.975
The seven are Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe and Australia.

00:01:10.205 --> 00:01:13.853
Find each on the world map before memorising descriptions.

00:01:14.083 --> 00:01:21.145
The book also recalls Pangaea, the ancient joined land mass, to show that the continents have moved over immense time.

00:01:21.895 --> 00:01:23.644
Read the map from west to east.

00:01:23.874 --> 00:01:27.565
The two Americas lie between the Pacific and Atlantic Oceans.

00:01:27.795 --> 00:01:30.611
Europe and Africa lie east of the Atlantic.

00:01:30.841 --> 00:01:37.945
Asia stretches far across the Northern Hemisphere, with the Pacific on its east; Australia lies to its southeast.

00:01:38.175 --> 00:01:40.308
Antarctica circles the far south.

00:01:41.058 --> 00:01:46.242
The important skill is location and relationship, not just reciting seven names.

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:50.141
Trace the Atlantic between the Americas and Europe and Africa.

00:01:50.371 --> 00:01:54.233
Then trace the Indian Ocean south of Asia and east of Africa.

00:01:54.463 --> 00:01:58.345
Use these positions to check the continent descriptions that follow.

00:01:59.095 --> 00:02:03.725
On a world map, which ocean lies between the Americas and Europe and Africa?

00:02:11.955 --> 00:02:13.320
The Atlantic Ocean.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:20.956
Asia is the largest continent and occupies roughly a third of the world's land area in the textbook's account.

00:02:21.186 --> 00:02:23.767
It joins Europe in the Eurasian land mass.

00:02:23.997 --> 00:02:28.883
The Ural Mountains, Black Sea and Caspian Sea help mark their conventional division.

00:02:29.113 --> 00:02:33.635
The Arctic is to Asia's north, the Pacific east and the Indian Ocean south.

00:02:34.385 --> 00:02:36.241
Africa is second largest.

00:02:36.471 --> 00:02:39.927
The Mediterranean Sea lies between Africa and Europe.

00:02:40.157 --> 00:02:44.339
The Indian Ocean is to Africa's east and the Atlantic to its west.

00:02:44.569 --> 00:02:53.806
The Sahara, across northern Africa, is the world's largest hot desert; on your map, find it above the equator and below the Mediterranean coast.

00:02:54.556 --> 00:02:58.481
Europe is smaller, with a very irregular or indented coastline.

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:02.978
Its bays and peninsulas create many places for harbours and ports.

00:03:03.208 --> 00:03:06.557
The Arctic is to its north and the Atlantic to its west.

00:03:06.787 --> 00:03:12.355
When you compare these three continents, use both size and position to make the answer meaningful.

00:03:13.855 --> 00:03:16.799
North America is the third largest continent.

00:03:17.029 --> 00:03:20.485
The Pacific lies to its west and the Atlantic to its east.

00:03:20.715 --> 00:03:25.323
A narrow land link, the Isthmus of Panama, joins it to South America.

00:03:25.553 --> 00:03:33.511
South America is fourth largest, is roughly triangular, and has the same ocean pattern: Pacific west, Atlantic east.

00:03:34.261 --> 00:03:38.293
Antarctica is a vast frozen land mass around the South Pole.

00:03:38.523 --> 00:03:46.608
Its ice, harsh climate and surrounding seas make ordinary settlement very difficult, but scientists work at research stations there.

00:03:46.838 --> 00:03:52.854
Do not confuse its ice-covered land with the Arctic, which is an ocean region around the North Pole.

00:03:53.604 --> 00:03:59.940
Australia is the smallest continent and is often called an island continent because water surrounds it.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:02.538
It lies south of the main Asian land mass.

00:04:02.768 --> 00:04:13.307
Now close the map and rebuild it: the Americas to the west, Europe and Africa near the Atlantic's east, Asia farther east, Australia southeast, Antarctica below.

00:04:14.057 --> 00:04:17.299
What narrow land link joins North and South America?

00:04:25.529 --> 00:04:27.129
The Isthmus of Panama.

00:04:27.359 --> 00:04:29.663
An isthmus is a narrow land bridge.

00:04:30.643 --> 00:04:35.977
A mountain is a great rise in Earth's crust, often appearing in a long chain called a range.

00:04:36.207 --> 00:04:41.455
To understand how mountains form, picture the crust broken into enormous tectonic plates.

00:04:41.685 --> 00:04:43.861
They move very slowly over the mantle.

00:04:44.091 --> 00:04:50.683
Their movement can squeeze, fracture or lift rock, and some plate boundaries also allow molten rock to rise.

00:04:51.433 --> 00:04:55.913
Where crustal layers are squeezed together, compression can bend them into folds.

00:04:56.143 --> 00:05:00.900
Where rock breaks, the fracture is a fault, and blocks of land can move up or down.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:06.634
At some boundaries one plate moves beneath another; that downward movement is called subduction.

00:05:07.384 --> 00:05:09.219
These are internal processes.

00:05:09.449 --> 00:05:17.107
The mountains we see today also have a long external history: rain, rivers, ice and wind wear peaks and slopes down.

00:05:17.337 --> 00:05:21.497
So height and shape tell us something about both construction and erosion.

00:05:21.727 --> 00:05:26.782
Next, compare the three mountain types in your chapter: fold, block and volcanic.

00:05:28.282 --> 00:05:31.205
Watch the diagram of horizontal rock layers.

00:05:31.435 --> 00:05:35.616
If powerful forces push from opposite sides, the layers are compressed.

00:05:35.846 --> 00:05:37.787
They bend rather than staying flat.

00:05:38.017 --> 00:05:41.580
That folding can lift long belts of rock into fold mountains.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:46.525
The key words for an answer are layers of crust, compression, folding and uplift.

00:05:47.275 --> 00:05:50.155
Do not mistake a folded drawing for a single hill.

00:05:50.385 --> 00:05:55.526
Real fold mountain systems extend over large distances and form ranges.

00:05:55.756 --> 00:06:06.423
The Himalayas in Asia, Andes in South America, Rockies in North America and Alps in Europe are examples named in the chapter's discussion of young fold mountains.

00:06:07.173 --> 00:06:16.431
Try explaining the diagram without the labels: point to the two inward arrows, follow one coloured layer as it bends, and identify the raised ridge.

00:06:16.661 --> 00:06:20.608
A diagram answer is stronger when its words match the movement shown.

00:06:20.838 --> 00:06:25.531
Compression makes folds; it does not mean the rocks simply pile up like loose sand.

00:06:26.281 --> 00:06:29.631
What force bends the rock layers that form a fold mountain?

00:06:37.861 --> 00:06:40.570
Compression: pressure pushing the layers together.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:47.630
The chapter separates old and young fold mountains by their long history of formation and erosion.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:53.343
Old fold ranges have been worn down for much longer, so they are generally lower and gentler.

00:06:53.573 --> 00:06:59.781
The Aravallis in India, Appalachians in North America and Urals in Russia are the book's examples.

00:07:00.531 --> 00:07:04.755
Young fold mountains formed more recently in geological time.

00:07:04.985 --> 00:07:08.163
Their ridges are often higher, steeper and more rugged.

00:07:08.393 --> 00:07:17.375
The Himalayas, Andes, Rockies and Alps are familiar examples; the textbook also lists the Atlas Mountains and Great Dividing Range.

00:07:17.605 --> 00:07:23.301
Young does not mean new in a human lifetime: even millions of years is young in geology.

00:07:24.051 --> 00:07:26.995
A common mistake is to answer only with names.

00:07:27.225 --> 00:07:34.670
Explain the reason for the difference: a longer time of weathering and erosion tends to lower and soften older ranges.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:41.833
Then give one old and one young example, and if shown photographs, look at slope and height with that process in mind.

00:07:43.333 --> 00:07:44.763
Fold mountains bend.

00:07:44.993 --> 00:07:48.235
Block mountains form when the crust fractures along faults.

00:07:48.465 --> 00:07:54.588
In the chapter's diagram, the land between two faults sinks, while the blocks on either side remain higher.

00:07:54.818 --> 00:08:00.173
Those steep-edged higher blocks are block mountains, and the lower central strip is a rift valley.

00:08:00.923 --> 00:08:03.099
Trace one layer across the diagram.

00:08:03.329 --> 00:08:08.918
It is broken and displaced at the faults, instead of being smoothly bent as in a fold mountain.

00:08:09.148 --> 00:08:11.921
That difference is the most important comparison.

00:08:12.151 --> 00:08:19.191
A fault is the break; a block mountain is a raised or standing section; a rift valley is a down-dropped section.

00:08:19.941 --> 00:08:26.640
The textbook gives the Vosges and Black Forest in Europe and the Vindhyas in India as block mountain examples.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:31.115
Learn at least one and practise drawing the two faults with a lower middle block.

00:08:31.345 --> 00:08:35.996
Label the block mountains, rift valley and faults, then explain which part moved.

00:08:36.746 --> 00:08:41.247
In the block-mountain diagram, what is the lower strip between two faults called?

00:08:49.477 --> 00:08:50.629
A rift valley.

00:08:50.859 --> 00:08:53.398
The higher blocks beside it are block mountains.

00:08:54.378 --> 00:08:56.213
Now picture a different process.

00:08:56.443 --> 00:08:58.853
Molten rock below the surface is magma.

00:08:59.083 --> 00:09:01.857
It can rise through a crack and passage called a vent.

00:09:02.087 --> 00:09:04.647
When it reaches the surface, we call it lava.

00:09:04.877 --> 00:09:11.938
Repeated eruptions of lava, ash, cinder and dust can build a volcanic mountain, often shaped like a cone.

00:09:12.688 --> 00:09:21.051
In the labelled cross-section, find the magma chamber deep below, the vent leading upward, and the crater, the bowl-like opening near the summit.

00:09:21.281 --> 00:09:22.987
These are three different parts.

00:09:23.217 --> 00:09:28.892
The crater is not the whole mountain, and lava is not the same word as magma beneath the surface.

00:09:29.642 --> 00:09:36.554
The chapter names Fuji in Japan, Mayon in the Philippines, Kilimanjaro in Tanzania and Vesuvius in Italy.

00:09:36.784 --> 00:09:42.459
Not every volcano has the same shape or activity, but the diagram gives you a clear model.

00:09:42.689 --> 00:09:47.083
Practise drawing it from memory, with arrows showing molten material rising.

00:09:48.583 --> 00:09:50.695
Mountains are more than peaks on a map.

00:09:50.925 --> 00:09:53.165
Glaciers and snow help feed rivers.

00:09:53.395 --> 00:09:57.961
Reservoirs supply irrigation water and can generate hydroelectricity.

00:09:58.191 --> 00:10:01.028
Mountain valleys and terraces support some farming.

00:10:01.258 --> 00:10:09.928
Forests provide habitats, fuel and other materials; mineral deposits support industry, and scenery attracts visitors and recreation.

00:10:10.678 --> 00:10:12.875
Mountains also influence climate.

00:10:13.105 --> 00:10:19.015
The Himalayas help block cold winds from Central Asia and affect the path of moist monsoon winds.

00:10:19.245 --> 00:10:23.853
Slopes, altitude and exposure make life and farming different from the plains.

00:10:24.083 --> 00:10:30.547
These benefits come with challenges, including difficult transport, fragile habitats and natural hazards.

00:10:31.297 --> 00:10:33.579
Before part two, test the whole chain.

00:10:33.809 --> 00:10:35.772
Name the seven continents on a map.

00:10:36.002 --> 00:10:40.930
Tell me how compression differs from faulting, then label magma, vent and crater.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.296
Finally give three distinct reasons mountains matter.

00:10:44.526 --> 00:10:49.454
If a label or cause feels uncertain, replay that diagram and explain it aloud.

00:10:50.204 --> 00:10:54.321
Explain one clear difference between a fold mountain and a block mountain.

00:11:02.551 --> 00:11:10.338
Fold mountains form when layers bend under compression; block mountains form when crust fractures and blocks move along faults.
